From dd629131fe32e75ff0976dafecad87ef68f9eb13 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CMGS <ilskdw@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 19:04:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] vmm: Reuse the copy-on-write restore source in snapshots

A VM restored with memory_restore_mode=copyonwrite maps its RAM
privately from the snapshot memory file. A snapshot of that VM writes
all guest memory again. Most pages are still equal to the source file.

Clone the source into the new memory file with FICLONERANGE, for the
length of the snapshot. Then write only the private copies that the
pagemap reader finds. The result is a complete memory file, thus
restore does not change.

Open the pagemap when the copy-on-write mapping is created. Landlock
applies after that, thus the snapshot needs no new rule.

Write all guest memory, as before, if the memory layout changed since
the restore, if the pagemap cannot be opened, or if the clone fails
(different filesystem, no reflink support). The last two log a warning.
Allow FICLONERANGE in the seccomp filter of the VMM thread.

8 GiB guest on XFS, restored copy-on-write, idle for 3 minutes:

  bytes written                      8 GiB  -> 274 MB
  vm.snapshot + fsync of the file    17.2 s -> 0.9 s

Signed-off-by: CMGS <ilskdw@gmail.com>
